  "result": "I wrote a revised record for `drv.motion.right_turn_from_onset` to `record.jsonl`. It has not been coded or gated yet. That happens next, then the review runs again.\n\n**What the review caught:** the coder's last body found the moment the turn is recognized by checking whether the heading-change threshold was just crossed. It compared the current observation with the one before. That earlier observation can belong to a previous turning stretch, measured from an older start. With the turn angle set to zero, a new stretch that starts at t=750 should be recognized right there, since its heading change is 0. The old body missed it because the threshold had already been met at t=500, counted from the old start.\n\n**What I changed in the record:**\n- **Recognition wording:** the turn is now recognized at the first observation of the current stretch, the start included, where the heading change reaches the negative of the turn angle. If the zero change at the start already meets it, the start is the recognition point. A heading change measured before the start, from an earlier start, has no bearing.\n- **The `act.recognition` field** now carries those same words.\n- **A new element for recognition**, which reads `drv.motion.turning_right` and `kin.yaw_rate`. Its justification spells out how a correct body should test it: the threshold is met at the start of `turning_right`, or at a later observation where it is met and was not met earlier in the same stretch. It must never compare against the observation before the start.\n\nEverything else is as before: the formals, parameters, the other two elements, the three scenarios (`blind` still masks `kin.yaw_rate`), `used_by` and `differs_from`.\n\n`drv.motion.left_turn_from_onset` has the same wording, and a body written the same way would have the same flaw. It is already accepted, so I didn't touch it here; it would need its own repair.",
